      en: "Sanctuary Bay in Freeport, Grand Bahama, is a world-class marine landmark and a pioneer in the study and interaction with Atlantic bottlenose dolphins. Established in the 1980s, the facility is home to a resident pod of dolphins that live in a large, naturally flushed saltwater lagoon. Sanctuary Bay is most famous for its 'Dolphin Experience,' which offers visitors a unique and educational opportunity to swim and interact with these highly intelligent marine animals in a safe and respectful environment. The facility is a major center for marine research and conservation, playing a vital role in national biodiversity education. For decades, it has been a favorite destination for families and researchers, offering a unique perspective on the marine life of the Bahamas. The bay is situated in a scenic coastal area, providing a sense of continuity and natural beauty. Today, Sanctuary Bay remains a symbol of the nation's commitment to marine preservation and environmental education. Its preservation is a national priority, ensuring that this important piece of natural heritage is honored. It is a global leader in marine mammal interaction."
